Problem description
- There is a finite planning horizon of $$T$$ periods, and a set of products $$P$$. Each product is produced in standardized batches, and fractional batch quantities are allowed.
- There is a set of renewable resources $$R$$. In the original case, these resources are automated machine time and manual craftsman time, but the model is written generically for any resource set.
- Each product $$p \in P$$ consumes a known amount of each resource $$r \in R$$ per batch, given by the processing-time parameter $$a_{p,r}$$.
- Each period $$t \in T$$ has a known available capacity of each resource $$r \in R$$, given by the capacity parameter $$b_{r,t}$$.
- Each product $$p \in P$$ generates a known unit revenue $$u_p$$ per batch sold.
- Each resource $$r \in R$$ has a known operating cost rate $$c_r$$ per hour. Idle resource time has no cost.
- A time conversion factor $$\kappa$$ is used when processing times and capacities are expressed in different time units.
- All produced batches are assumed to be sold, so production quantities and sales quantities coincide, and there is no inventory carryover.
- A designated product $$p^{\mathrm{c}} \in P$$ must satisfy a minimum contract quantity in every period, and the required minimum quantity is given by $$L_t$$.
- If market demand limits are provided, they act as upper bounds on how much of each product can be sold in each period; otherwise demand is assumed sufficiently large and nonbinding.
- The planning goal is to choose the production quantities over the horizon so that total profit, defined as total revenue minus total resource operating cost, is maximized.
- A solution is represented by:
  - $$x_{p,t}$$: the number of standardized batches of product $$p \in P$$ produced in period $$t \in T$$, with fractional values allowed.
  - $$\Pi$$: the resulting total profit.
  - Optionally, the implied resource usage by resource and period.

Input Format
{
  "P": "$$P$$, number of products",
  "R": "$$R$$, number of renewable resource types",
  "T": "$$T$$, number of planning periods",
  "products": "$$\\{0,1,\\ldots,P-1\\}$$, product index set",
  "resources": "$$\\{0,1,\\ldots,R-1\\}$$, resource index set",
  "periods": "$$\\{0,1,\\ldots,T-1\\}$$, period index set",
  "time_conversion_factor": "$$\\kappa$$, number of minutes per hour",
  "processing_time": "$$a_{p,r}$$ with shape $$P\\times R$$, minutes of resource $$r$$ required per batch of product $$p$$",
  "available_time": "$$b_{r,t}$$ with shape $$R\\times T$$, available time of resource $$r$$ in period $$t$$, expressed in hours",
  "unit_revenue": "$$u_p$$ with shape $$P$$, revenue per batch of product $$p$$",
  "resource_cost_per_hour": "$$c_r$$ with shape $$R$$, operating cost per hour of resource $$r$$",
  "market_demand": "$$d_{p,t}$$ with shape $$P\\times T$$, optional upper bound on sellable quantity of product $$p$$ in period $$t$$; if omitted, demand is assumed nonbinding",
  "contract_product": "$$p^{\\mathrm{c}} \\in P$$, index of the product subject to the minimum contract requirement",
  "minimum_contract_quantity": "$$L_t$$ with shape $$T$$, minimum quantity of product $$p^{\\mathrm{c}}$$ that must be produced in period $$t$$"
}

Output Format
{
  "production_plan": "$$x_{p,t}$$ with shape $$P\\times T$$, nonnegative real-valued production quantities for every product and period",
  "total_profit": "$$\\Pi$$, scalar objective value representing maximum profit"
}

